/*
    `^^` is `^` with its operands reversed, so `A ^^ B` denotes `B ^ A` and it is defined
    wherever `^` is.

    Its principal use is a convenient way to define the set of functions from the 
    left operand set to the right operand: `int ^^ string` is the set of functions 
    from `int` to `string`
    
    It is right-associative, so `A ^^ B ^^ C` is the curried `A ^^ (B ^^ C)`.
    `A ^^ B` includes both total and partial functions; use `total (A ^^ B)` where
    definedness over the admitted domain is required.
*/

int ^^ string
